now the public and commercial services  union took the unprecedented step of  taking part in legal action against the  government over its policy to remove  asylum seekers to rwanda and it's of  course the job of civil servants to  implement the policy putting them on a  collision course with the government we  can talk now to the pcs union general  secretary at mark sawatka who joins us  and now from wales i believe and good to  uh see you on the programme and before  we get to the rwanda case i just want to  talk to you about story in the sunday  telegraph today grant shaps giving an  interview where he says agency workers  could be brought in to effectively break  strikes to cover uh the jobs of striking  workers that would be changing  the law to remove that restriction  what's your reaction  well that would be an outrageous act if  the government go down that road we've  already got the most restrictive  anti-union legislation in the whole of  western europe it's already incredibly  difficult for unions and workers to  withdraw their labor there are so many  legal loopholes to fly through and if  you think about what grant chaps is  trying to do with that legislation is to  make industrial action ineffective and i  believe it's a basic human right to be  able to withdraw your labor you only do  it as a last resort and any attempts to  bring people in to undermine disputes  will not resolve the problems at the end  of the day a dispute happens because  there's disagreement between the  employer and the workforce that  disagreement is not going to be resolved  if people can be brought in to undermine  disputes it will lead to bitterness it  will lead to division and it will lead  to these problems getting worse so what  grandchat should really be doing is  trying to resolve the dispute to ensure  our rail workers frontline public sector  workers have decent paying conditions  they're not threatened with the cutting  their cost of living standards and he  should try to resolve the dispute not  poor petrol on the flames and make  matters worse  you know you say that you know strikes  are the last resort for workers and i  just wonder if you know the government  might feel that this kind of changed the  law is a last resort for itself too  and you know we've heard from the rnt  saying that they're going to call  they're going to call  an unknown number of strikes that could  go on for an unknown number of days or  weeks over the summer it could  potentially haven't ruled out  you know lead to a situation where the  lights go off and do you worry that the  unions might have actually overplayed  their hand a little bit here and that's  why we could be seeing this pushback  from the government  no i think my own view is probably the  unions have underplayed their hand now  over far too many years you know we  we've seen right across trade unions in  the public and private sector terms and  conditions eroded in the civil service  for example we've seen 11 consecutive  years of real terms pay cuts  and the fact that it's so difficult now  to organize lawful industrial action  means all the cards are stacked in the  favor of the government or unscrupulous  employers as we've recently seen with  pno ferries who just similarly dismissed  800 workers and brought people in at  deplorably low levels of pay so the  point of industrial action is that it  gives workers collectively the chance to  stand together to try and get a better  deal  and i think that we have a long and  proud history of the right to strike of  the right of people to defend themselves  as a last resort  and what i would hope that your viewers  would understand sophie is when people  go on strike they lose pay it's the last  thing that they want to do but if a  company is insisting on cutting your pay  cutting your pension cutting your job  for example and they will not listen to  reason it's the last thing you can do  and of course if you're going to  withdraw your labor the point of doing  that is to have an effect to try to get  people back around the negotiating table  so this is a wrong move  on top of already incredibly restrictive  legislation and it will make matters  worse not resolve the problems that we  want to see resolved  uh we spoke to rachel reeves a little  earlier on the programme she refused to  go as far as we're streeting who said  that if he was a member of the rmt he'd  be voting for  strike action and i spoke to mick lynch  on the programme a couple of weeks ago  and he said that he effectively thought  that the labour party weren't showing  there on the side of workers he asked  the question what's the point  of the labour party  do you agree with mick lynch do you  think that their response has been a bit  too bland  well i would certainly like to see a lot  stronger support from the labour party  and of course many in the labour party  have been quite supportive you know  whether that's john mcdonnell or whether  that's leasing andy in recent weeks  but what the labour party are going to  have to do is come up with a position  because the way things are going we're  going to see more industrial action as  the year goes on because people are  facing such a catastrophic cut in their  living standards or in my case  representing people who have been told  one in five ninety one thousand of them  face losing their jobs they're being  told they're getting a 2 pay rise when  inflation is 9 to 11 depending what  index you use we saw the general  secretary of unison the other day say  that she was considering balloting  unison's 1.1 million members so the  labour party will have to decide whose  side they are on and given it was  founded by the trade unions and they are  there to speak up for working men and  women i would hope that we would see kia  stammer and his team fundamentally come  out on the side of nurses doctors job  center workers rail workers and  education workers that's what we want is  a political voice in parliament okay  stands with the people who are having to  take this  difficult stand now of course the pcs  union took the unusual action last week  of trying to stop the government's plan  to send asylum seekers to rwanda you  took court action which she lost the  judge the judge sorry found in favor  of the government to allow that flight  to rwanda to  go ahead  um are you going to be appealing the  decision and do you think that or do you  know except that this flight is going to  go ahead on tuesday  no we're appealing the decision we're in  high court tomorrow appealing the  decision  and as you say this is an unprecedented  step sophie but but let me explain why  we're doing it  we have to test the legality of these  proposals but also we need to debate  about the morality and lack of humanity  that the government is demonstrating  when people are fleeing from iraq or  iran or syria or yemen and they come to  this country they are fleeing death  torture and persecution  we should welcome people to this country  and treat them civilly and assess their  cases when they are here the fact that  people are taking to small boat  crossings in the channel shows how  desperate they are and our members civil  servants 80 of whom we represent in the  border force  don't come to work to be asked to turn  those boats back mid-channel which is  what pretty propel demanded that they  did two months ago and i remind viewers  we went to court then we lost two  injunctions but we would have won the  case which is why the home office  withdrew its policy and let's remember  what pretty patel wanted pcs members to  do was to turn dinghy's back mid-channel  at risk of death that was stopped by a  legal process and that's why we're  taking legal action now  and we hope we win tomorrow in the court  of appeal to stop the flight but of  course the legality of these proposals  will only be tested out at the full  court sharing in july and we're  absolutely confident that in july in  line with what the unhcr said very  graphically in court we believe these  proposals will be found to be unlawful  okay  now i just i i appreciate the sort of  strength of feeling that many will feel  many of our viewers as well about this  policy  i just want to read part of the civil  service code  it says you must not frustrate the  implementation of policies once  decisions are taken  you must serve the government whatever  its political persuasion to the best of  your ability in a way which maintains  political impartiality no matter what  your own political beliefs are  in seeking an injunction to try and stop  this flight you know you're effectively  trying to change and frustrate the  implementation of policy  many would argue that is breaching the  code  well many may argue that of course i  think the first part i would make is  when that civil service code was written  it was based on two fundamental premises  firstly that anything you were asked to  do as a single servant was lawful  we believe that this is not lawful and  the court has yet to determine that's  the case but secondly it presupposes  that the government of the day itself  abides by the rule of law and acts  within the rule of law we know this  government has shown demonstrably he has  not done that and what we saw if we can  just for a minute look at the partygate  proposals is when it came down to it  it's civil servants who were fined it's  civil servants who are now facing  disciplinary action while the prime  minister seems to get away scot-free so  given the government's disregard for the  rule of law and giving it to be fair the  courts have found in favor of the  government or you know the last week  well what they they've not found these  proposals legal what happened last week  was an injunction was refused the  legality of the proposals will not be  determined till july and sophie the  question i would respectfully ask is  imagine if you're a civil servant now in  the home office who goes to work because  you want to see a humane immigration  system where people are treated fairly  and we comply with our international  obligations imagine if you're told to do  something on tuesday that in july is  subsequently found to be illegal that  would be an appalling situation and if  pretty patel had any respect  not just for the desperate people who  come to this country but for the workers  she employs she would not ask a single  one of them to be part of any  deportation of any asylum seeker until  at least these cases are heard in court  for the full legality judgment  will be in july  just finally uh quickly um you rejoined  the labour party in 2016 to support  jeremy corbyn in transforming the party  are you still a member under kyoustama  yes i am okay uh thank you very much uh  indeed mark so walk there  thank you

